 bounded text replacement.","requiredRepairs":["Extend body to at least 600 words using the licensed evidence cards (65 distinct assertions cleared for 600-850 words); the current 370-word draft is ~230 words short of the gate minimum.","Correct the international revenue figure in the body lede and in the summary: all sources report $139.6M internationally (and $124.5M domestic) for a $264.1M worldwide total. The $200M figure in both the lede and the frontmatter summary is the pre-opening worldwide projection from early Thursday previews, not the final international split.","Add at least one ## heading that naturally expresses the demand lens (e.g. referencing Nolan's career-record global debut) per the demand-lens-missing-h2 warning.","Remove or source-anchor the unsupported analytical closure in 'Sustaining the Spectacle': the claims about sustainability of the event-cinema model and studio willingness to maintain capital-intensive distribution are not licensed by any admitted source assertion."],"repairTools":[],"report":"## Editorial Assessment ‚Äî reporting-source-3-for-odyssey-becomes-christopher-nolan-biggest\n\n### Pipeline Diagnosis\n\nThe earliest causal failure is the **writer-model layer**. The assignment is well-formed, the evidence budget was cleared (65 distinct licensed assertions supporting 600‚Äì850 words), and the sources (LA Times, Times of India, WinterIsComing, Deadline, Variety) collectively supply adequate warrant for a full article. The writer produced a 370-word draft ‚Äî 230 words short of the 600-word gate ‚Äî and introduced a material numerical error in the very first factual sentence. Neither defect was compelled by the sources or the instructions; both are writer execution failures. Replay is required from the writer layer; the assignment need not be reconsidered.\n\n---\n\n### Gate Audit\n\n**ERROR ‚Äî body-too-short (deterministic gate, copy-edited)**\nThe body is 370 words. The `current-news-brief/v1` commission requires a minimum of 600 words, and the evidence budget is cleared for 600‚Äì850. No bounded `replace_exact_body_text` operation can supply the missing ~230 words from licensed evidence without constituting a prose rewrite. This defect alone bars publication and bars the bounded-repair path.\n\n**WARNING ‚Äî demand-lens-missing-h2 (deterministic gate, standards-cleared)**\nThe three existing h2 headings ‚Äî \"A Technical and Cultural Reach,\" \"Market Performance and Competition,\" and \"Sustaining the Spectacle\" ‚Äî do not naturally express the demand lens (\"odyssey becomes christopher nolan biggest global debut million\"). The reporting plainly supports a heading that references Nolan's career-record global debut. The writer should add one.\n\n---\n\n### Material Claim Audit\n\n**Critical factual error ‚Äî international revenue figure (body lede and summary frontmatter)**\nThe body opens: \"The figures, which include approximately $200 million from international markets.\" The summary repeats the same figure.\n\nEvery admitted source is unambiguous: the international take was **$139.6 million**; the domestic take was $124.5 million; the worldwide total was $264.1 million. The $200 million figure appears only once in the evidence ‚Äî as a *pre-opening worldwide projection* from early Thursday previews and advance ticket sales (WinterIsComing: \"Early projections had the film grossing approximately $200 million at the worldwide box office following strong Thursday previews\"). The writer transposed this pre-release projection into the international column of the final result. This is a material numerical error in the article's first paragraph and in its summary.\n\n**Precision concern ‚Äî summer box office comparison**\nThe body states: \"summer box office revenue continues to lag behind 2019 levels by approximately 7% to 9%.\" The LA Times source actually reports two separate comparisons: approximately 7% *ahead* of the same period last year, and approximately 9% *behind* pre-pandemic 2019. Collapsing these into a single \"7% to 9% lag behind 2019\" elides the favorable year-over-year comparison and could mislead. The writer should preserve both figures with their respective referents.\n\n**Unsupported analytical closure ‚Äî \"Sustaining the Spectacle\" section**\nThe closing section introduces a speculative framing ‚Äî whether the event-cinema model is \"sustainable\" and whether major studios will \"maintain the capital-intensive distribution requirements these films demand\" ‚Äî that no admitted source assertion licenses. The LA Times quotes IMAX CEO Rich Gelfond on IMAX's growing importance to filmmakers and studios; that is the outer boundary of what the sources support on this theme. The writer has extrapolated from a quoted executive into an open editorial question not present in the reporting. Under the genre contract (reported analysis must remain subordinate to and licensed by the reporting), this section either needs source grounding or must be replaced with a supported closing fact ‚Äî such as the upcoming Spider-Man: Brand New Day competition on July 30‚Äì31, which multiple sources explicitly name as the next event facing the film.\n\n**Accurate claims verified against sources**\n- $264.1 million worldwide opening: corroborated by all five sources.\n- Dethrones The Dark Knight Rises ($249 million) as Nolan's biggest global debut: corroborated by Deadline, WinterIsComing, Times of India.\n- IMAX drove a substantial ticket-sales share; IMAX dedicated screens for three weeks: supported by Times of India and LA Times.\n- Gelfond's involvement in developing the quieter camera, easier reload, trained-projectionists requirement: attributed to LA Times, correctly.\n- Practical effects (puppetry, robotics, Viking boat): supported by LA Times.\n- 95% Rotten Tomatoes score: supported by Times of India.\n- France, Italy, Spain, Poland, the Netherlands, Brazil as Nolan's biggest-ever openings: supported by WinterIsComing citing Deadline.\n- Minimal direct competition opening weekend; Spider-Man: Brand New Day arrives July 30‚Äì31: supported by Times of India and WinterIsComing.\n\n**Attribution**\nThe body's inline links are present and grammatically integrated; no standalone citation drops were observed. The lead article URL is linked in the body. No title alteration of linked sources was detected. The \"As [X] notes/reports\" template construction is used once and within the allowed limit.\n\n---\n\n### Headline and Lede Faithfulness\n\nThe headline ‚Äî \"Nolan's Odyssey Reaches New Heights with $264.1M Global Debut\" ‚Äî is accurate and preserves the key figure. The lede is faithful to the event except for the critical international-figure error noted above.\n\n---\n\n### Recommendation\n\nThis manuscript requires a writer-model replay. The body must reach at least 600 words using the licensed evidence cards (the evidence budget is adequate). The international revenue figure must be corrected in both the body and the summary. At least one h2 should naturally express the demand lens. The speculative \"Sustaining the Spectacle\" closing should be anchored to licensed source assertions or replaced with a supported unresolved fact. The pipeline's sources, assignment, and instructions are sound; only the writer execution requires correction."}ÉdpairÉhconstantÇdbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;JMJth7qzQ_lo03_wzKDNvDikI29A9IRg5WGs8PYGltEÉdpairÉerightÅdunitÉerightÅdunitÉerightÅdunitÉerightÅdunitÉerightÅdunitÉerightÅdunitÉdleftÅdstarÅdunitÇdmarkx9ni:///sha-256;VFZjJj1jXlkwIQwJNBKlXmZcKsBMl5LHWbXU92VcbxEÉdpairÉhconstantÇdbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;OgyCriaTOqDg2Bs7_0ZjcrMgYodiuMTGj5Jhos0DVJIÉdpairÉdleftÅdstarÉcsumÅdunitÉcsumÅdunitÉcsumÅdunitÉcsumÅdunitÉcsumÅdunitÉcsumÅdunitÅdunitÇdmark`ÉdpairÉhconstantÇdbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;c06HOuDSGUZ9CWxoFwlJLXkoHEK3eCZDH3VVZuFLOUQÉdpairÉerightÅdunitÉerightÅdunitÉerightÅdunitÉerightÅdunitÉerightÅdunitÉerightÅdunitÉdleftÅdstarÅdunitÇdmarkxclaude-code-cli-invocationsÉdpairÉhco